own as  pcr and that's all done  in this little portable box right so  yeah the idea is to really post a  patient  and the and the cartridges are they  reusable or they they only can be used  once  no they're disposable because obviously  we don't want to contaminate  in everything so once you've done the  test you throw them away  and how long has it taken you to develop  this capability  well actually we repurposed it from  something that we're using in retail  anyway for genetic testing the original  idea  was to not look at for the genes of the  virus but to look at human dna  and to test things like risks for  obesity and type 2 diabetes  which as you know are epidemic today  and effectively and then you can shop  with your dna we've got a wearable and  you can go and shop and  pre-covered we thought to ourselves well  actually if we take the same technology  and instead of putting  the bait for human dna why don't we put  the bait  for the viral rna and that's what we did  so it took us from the time we invented  it  for dna testing to the time that we're  using it now  for virus testing it took us about four  or five months of  flat out repurposing from about march up  until now  so it's been really really hard work  we've worked with the department of  health we've validated in  in major london hospitals particularly  saint mary's  also at the john radcliffe in oxford  we've got great  sensitivity and one of the important um  value-added functions is because we've  got so many wells  of many things we mentioned flu flu a  flu b  rsv but also we can look at human  dna which means we're testing the  adequacy of the swab  so if the swap was no good we would know  because we wouldn't get a result so it  would be an invalid  whereas in hospitals unfortunately they  don't have that control  and some of these results come back as  negative when really  the swab hasn't been adequate so that's  the beauty of a test like this with no  lab as well  and how much will it